  <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6 mb-8">
    <h2>1. The 3 Mechanisms of Heat Transfer in an Attic</h2>
    <p>
      To understand why a radiant barrier requires an airspace, you must examine how heat moves from a sunny summer sky into your living room:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Conduction:</strong> Direct microscopic molecule-to-molecule heat transfer through solid materials. Solar heat conducts through asphalt shingles into the OSB roof sheathing.</li>
      <li><strong>Convection:</strong> Heat transfer through the movement of fluids or gases. Hot air rises toward the roof peak and exhausts out the ridge vent.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiation:</strong> Heat transferred via invisible infrared electromagnetic waves travelling across an open void. The hot bottom surface of the OSB roof deck (often 150°F to 160°F) acts like an incandescent heat lamp, radiating thermal waves straight down across the open attic air onto your fiberglass insulation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Conventional attic insulation (fiberglass batt or blown cellulose) is designed to resist <em>conduction and convection</em>, but is virtually transparent to <em>radiant heat waves</em>. The radiant barrier foil reflects 97% of these electromagnetic waves back upward before they can ever touch the fiberglass insulation.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Why an Open Airspace is Physically Mandatory</h2>
    <p>
      Thermal radiation can <strong>only occur across an open, transparent void</strong> (an airspace). If you sandwich a radiant barrier directly between the roof rafters and plywood sheathing with zero airspace, heat transfers through the aluminum via direct <em>conduction</em>. Because aluminum is one of the best thermal conductors on earth, touching the sheathing eliminates 100% of its radiant benefit!
    </p>
    <p>
      To reflect heat, the low-emissivity aluminum surface must face an open airspace of at least <strong>3/4 inch</strong> (and ideally the full depth of a 2x4 or 2x6 rafter cavity).
    </p>

    <h2>3. Rafter Stapling vs. Attic Floor Placement Comparison</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Method</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Dust Degradation Risk</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Winter Moisture Threat</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Attic Air Temperature Impact</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Building Science Verdict</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Stapled Under Rafters (Facing Down)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Zero (Dust falls downward away from foil)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Safe (Ventilated rafter channel vents moisture)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Attic stays 25°F–30°F cooler (Protects HVAC ducts)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">RECOMMENDED (Best Practice)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Laid Flat on Attic Floor Insulation</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-red-600 font-bold">Extreme (Dust destroys emissivity in 2 years)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-red-600 font-bold">High (Traps winter exfiltrating household vapor)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Attic remains 150°F+ (Bakes HVAC ducts)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-red-600 font-bold">NOT RECOMMENDED</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Radiant Barrier OSB (Factory Glued Foil)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Zero (Foil faces downward into attic)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Safe (Requires proper soffit/ridge venting)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Attic stays 20°F–25°F cooler</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">EXCELLENT (New Construction)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. The Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) Dust Study</h2>
    <p>
      Extensive research by the U.S. Department of Energy at the Oak Ridge National Laboratory tested the long-term degradation of radiant barriers placed horizontally across attic floor insulation:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Brand new, clean aluminum foil exhibits an emissivity (&epsilon;) of <strong>0.03</strong> (it emits only 3% of radiant heat and reflects 97%).</li>
      <li>After 12 months on an attic floor, settling airborne dust particles increased emissivity to <strong>0.25</strong>.</li>
      <li>After 36 months, a 0.5 mm layer of domestic dust increased emissivity to <strong>0.75+</strong>, rendering the material <em>almost completely ineffective</em> as a radiant barrier.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      By contrast, foil stapled to the underside of roof rafters hangs vertically or at an angle facing downward. Gravity prevents airborne dust particles from clinging to the downward-facing reflective surface, allowing the barrier to maintain its 0.03 emissivity for decades.
    </p>
  </div>

